\description{
Compute observation residuals, element/overall \code{Infit} and \code{Outfit} (\code{MnSq}
and \code{ZSTD}), \code{PTMEA}, model/real SEs, facet separation/reliability/strata,
fixed/random facet chi-square screens, inter-rater agreement, unexpected
responses, fair averages, displacement, and optional marginal-fit or residual
PCA diagnostics for an ordinary \code{RSM}/\code{PCM} or one-family \code{GPCM}
\code{\link[=fit_mfrm]{fit_mfrm()}} object.
}
\details{
\strong{Fit, SE, and separation conventions.} MML residuals plug in Person EAPs;
legacy JML residuals use optimizer coordinates (\code{PersonMeasureBasis};
variances \verb{<= 1e-10} withhold standardized residuals, and any unavailable
contributing row sets element \code{MnSq}/\code{df} to \code{NA} via \code{FitStatus}/\code{FitReason}).
\code{RealSE = ModelSE * sqrt(max(Infit, 1))} inflates \code{ModelSE} for underfit
(\code{Fixed = TRUE} anchors keep \code{NA} SEs and suppress aggregate facet
reliability). Wright and Masters (1982) separation indices use sample \code{SD}
(\code{n - 1}) and \code{RMSE} across finite free levels (\code{TrueSD = sqrt(max(SD^2 - RMSE^2, 0))}, \eqn{G = \mathrm{TrueSD}/\mathrm{RMSE}},
\eqn{R = G^2/(1 + G^2)}, \eqn{H = (4G + 1)/3}); rater separation measures
severity spread rather than inter-rater agreement.

Read \code{decision} and \code{key_warnings}, then use the tables below to locate
results needing investigation. The summary reuses diagnostics; it does not
refit or promote a restricted source fit to inferential use.

\code{plot()} method: Plot saved ordinary-model diagnostics

Inspect an existing diagnostic result to locate cells or elements needing
follow-up. The selected display retains unavailable results and screening limits.
}
\section{Interpreting results}{

\itemize{
\item \code{Infit}/\code{Outfit} have model-reference value 1. Values above 1 describe
excess residual variation; values below 1 describe greater predictability.
\code{InfitZSTD}/\code{OutfitZSTD} standardize those mean squares; they are not
effect sizes or calibrated tests after fitting and repeated screening.
\code{top_fit} ranks only rows with both ZSTD values available. Its absence of
a level does not establish acceptable fit; inspect \code{object$fit} and
\code{FitReason} for unavailable standardizations.
\item \code{Reliability}/\code{Separation} describe how spread out facet measures are
relative to their estimated error. \code{RealReliability}/\code{RealSeparation}
use fit-inflated SEs, not lower confidence limits. High rater separation
can indicate severity differences, not agreement. Read \code{ReliabilityBasis},
\code{ReliabilityUse} and the computation details in \code{\link[=diagnose_mfrm]{diagnose_mfrm()}}.
\item \code{fit_screening} distinguishes classified from unclassified elements.
A known cutoff crossing remains flagged if another statistic is missing;
missing evidence is not a pass. Flags guide review, not automatic deletion.
\item \code{diagnostic_basis} distinguishes EAP plug-in response residuals from
posterior-integrated category-count screens. They answer different fit
questions; neither label alone authorizes a hypothesis test.
\item In \code{overview}, \code{Observations} counts retained rating rows and \code{Persons}
counts respondents. Repeated ratings of one Person do not create
additional independent Persons.
}
}

\section{Choosing a view}{

For \code{plot()}:

Marginal fit and pairwise plots require strict marginal diagnostics from
\code{\link[=diagnose_mfrm]{diagnose_mfrm()}} with \code{diagnostic_mode = "both"} or \code{"marginal_fit"} for
supported MML RSM/PCM models. Positive residuals indicate more observed
category usage or agreement than expected; negative values indicate less.
Returned \code{full_table} and \code{retention} distinguish omitted and unavailable
rows. A flag is a descriptive review rule, not a calibrated test.

Bubble plots place measures against mean-square fit, or Infit against Outfit.
Bubble size can represent SE or observation count; neither a review band
nor bubble size alone justifies deleting an element.

The PCA convenience route can derive PCA from the diagnostic result.
For repeated PCA displays, first save \code{\link[=analyze_residual_pca]{analyze_residual_pca()}} and use its
\code{plot()} method. Parallel-reference generation remains an analysis step.
}

\section{Expected point-measure correlation under ordinary JML}{

For ordinary additive RSM/PCM JML without group-mean anchors,
\code{measures$PTMEAExpected} adds a conditional reference beside observed
\code{PTMEA}. Let \code{x} be the oriented generating measure, \code{e} the expected score,
\code{v} its conditional model variance, and \code{N} the sum of frequency weights
within the element. The reference is
\deqn{\frac{\operatorname{Cov}_w(x,e)}{
\sqrt{\operatorname{Var}_w(x)\{\operatorname{Var}_w(e)+(N-2)\bar v_w/N\}}}.}
Weighted moments use the same observations and normalized frequency weights.
\code{PTMEAExpectedAsymptotic} omits the \code{(N-2)/N} adjustment. The correction is
Linacre's published finite-count reporting approximation; it is not an exact
expectation of the sample correlation, a significance test or a confidence
interval. Calibration is held fixed. Different administered PCM scales are
retained when calculating each observation's expectation and variance.

\code{PTMEAExpectedN}, \code{PTMEAExpectedFactor}, \code{PTMEAExpectedStatus} and
\code{PTMEAExpectedReason} identify the calculation and unavailable states.
At \code{N = 2} the variance term vanishes; this does not establish perfect
measurement or fit. Below \code{N = 2}, the corrected reference is unavailable
(FACETS 4.5.0 can instead display a boundary value). The asymptotic reference
remains available if its moments exist. Arbitrarily normalizing weights
changes the corrected reference: use replication semantics for comparison.
Constant predictors, invalid moments, or observations involving unanchored
extreme main effects withhold the complete element's expected reference;
they are not silently dropped. Fixed finite anchors remain usable.

FACETS 4.5.0 fixed-calibration PCM checks cover missing assignments and
unit, integer, fractional and unequal replication weights. These checks
establish a reporting convention within that scope, not general diagnostic
calibration. Observed \code{PTMEA} retains its existing definition. MML, corrected
JML, GPCM, fitted interactions and group-mean anchors do not receive the new
expected-correlation columns. Recreate older diagnostics from the saved fit
to obtain them; old saved results are not relabeled or silently recalculated.
See Linacre (2008), \emph{The Expected Value of a Point-Biserial (or Similar)
Correlation}, Rasch Measurement Transactions, 22:1, 1154, including its later
finite-count note: \url{https://www.rasch.org/rmt/rmt221e.htm}.
}

\examples{
# Long-running workflow; run explicitly when needed.
# example("diagnose_mfrm", run.dontrun = TRUE)
\dontrun{
# Load the package and example ratings
library(mfrmr)
ratings <- load_mfrmr_data("example_operational")

# Fit the model
fit <- fit_mfrm(
  data = ratings,
  person = "Person",
  facets = c("Rater", "Criterion"),
  score = "Score",
  rating_min = 1, rating_max = 4, category_policy = "preserve",
  method = "MML",
  model = "RSM"
)

# Check model fit and the support for standard errors and intervals
diagnostics <- diagnose_mfrm(fit)
diagnostic_summary <- summary(diagnostics)
diagnostic_summary$decision

diagnostic_summary$key_warnings # Issues to investigate, if present
diagnostic_summary$top_fit      # Most unusual residual-based fit statistics

# Distinguish residual-based checks from marginal model checks
diagnostic_summary$diagnostic_basis[, c("DiagnosticPath", "Status", "Basis")]

# Check each residual's basis and the coverage of aggregate fit statistics
head(diagnostics$obs[, c("PersonMeasureBasis", "ConditionalVariance",
  "PearsonAvailable", "ResponseReason")])
diagnostics$overall_fit[, c("Infit", "Outfit", "FitUnavailableRows", "FitReason")]
}
library(mfrmr)

# Saved synthetic MML/RSM analysis of example_operational.
# For your own data, fit a model first; see ?fit_mfrm.
example_fit <- readRDS(system.file("examples", "operational-mml.rds", package = "mfrmr"))
fit <- example_fit$fit

# Check model fit and the support for standard errors and intervals
diagnostics <- example_fit$diagnostics
diagnostic_summary <- summary(diagnostics)
diagnostic_summary$decision

diagnostic_summary$key_warnings # Issues to investigate, if present
diagnostic_summary$top_fit      # Most unusual residual-based fit statistics

# Distinguish residual-based checks from marginal model checks
diagnostic_summary$diagnostic_basis[, c("DiagnosticPath", "Status", "Basis")]
# Long-running workflow; run explicitly when needed.
# example("diagnose_mfrm", run.dontrun = TRUE)
\dontrun{
# Each row is one bundled example rating; use ordinary JML for this reference
ratings <- load_mfrmr_data("example_core")
jml_fit <- fit_mfrm(ratings, "Person", c("Rater", "Criterion"), "Score",
                     method = "JML", model = "RSM")
jml_diagnostics <- diagnose_mfrm(jml_fit, residual_pca = "none",
                                 diagnostic_mode = "legacy")
head(jml_diagnostics$measures[, c("Facet", "Level", "PTMEA",
     "PTMEAExpected", "PTMEAExpectedN", "PTMEAExpectedStatus")])
# Compare observed PTMEA with its conditional reference; neither is a test
}
}
